{
  "kind": "tea",
  "teaId": "ruth-field",
  "text": "Widowed, broke, foreign, and she still says the most loyal sentence in the Bible.\n\nNaomi loses her husband. Then both her sons. She is left with two daughters in law in a country that isn't hers, and she tells them both to go home. Twice. She's not being cold, she's being honest. She's got nothing to give them.\n\nOrpah kisses her and goes. Nobody should drag Orpah, she did exactly what she was told.\n\nBut Ruth clave unto her. And she says, \"Whither thou goest, I will go. Thy people shall be my people, and thy God my God.\"\n\nThat's a vow with a curse attached. She means it.\n\nSo they walk into Bethlehem with nothing, right as barley harvest starts. And Ruth goes to work, gleaning the leftovers, because the law said the poor get to follow the reapers.\n\nAnd the text says her hap was to light on a field belonging to Boaz. Her happenstance. Sure. The book spends two more chapters showing you what that was.\n\nBoaz clocks her immediately. Tells his men not to touch her. Tells her to drink their water. Then quietly tells them to drop extra grain where she'll find it.\n\nShe goes home with about an ephah of barley. That is a wildly good day.\n\nChapters later, Boaz redeems the family at the town gate, and Ruth has a son named Obed. Obed's son is Jesse. Jesse's son is David.\n\nLoyalty is a flex.",
